// `x = 5` in a statement position is a rebind, and the frontend lowers it to the
// same `bind` a `val` emits. It used to fall to the expression reader, which has
// no assignment rule, and came back as
// `expression cannot be lowered to TIR: Assignment`, with no instruction for the
// value either.
//
// `take` is declared by the same source, so its load reads the declaration and
// answers int. The canonical TIR is suppressed if any instruction is `unknown`,
// so this golden also pins that the callee rule made the text reachable at all.
// One string literal: the gate reads the fixture's embedded source as a single
// literal and compiles it through `orbit frontend`.
import "../../compiler/tir/entry.orb"

fn main() {
    val source = "fn take(n: int) -> int { return n * 2 } fn main() -> int { var x = 1 x = take(x + 20) x = x + 1 return x }"
    print(compileFrontendSource("fixture", source))
}
